Evaluate desugared models over indexed tables

Desugared models now store each relation in a mutable table instead of
persistent prefix trees. Rows are stamped with their position in the
table's insertion log, and every rule input reads a stamp-bounded view
of a hash trie index or a sorted copy of new rows: a stratum's old rows
are those stamped before its previous read and its new rows are the
rest. Semi-naive evaluation thus needs neither snapshots nor structural
set differences.

Function tables report a second result for the same arguments when a
row is inserted. Evaluation equates such results after every stratum
instead of running generated functionality rules, and the ordinary rules
of a recursive component share one stratum per round.

Native models keep the persistent storage and morphism transport.
